Answer: For "most" values of $c$ and $B$, the Galois group of $x^c-(1-x)^c-B$ is not solvable, so in particular the equation $x^c-(1-x)^c=B$ can not (in general) be solved in radicals. 
 For a concrete example (suggested already in the comments), with $c=5$ and $B=2$, the Galois group is $S_5$. This is also the case for 
$$(c,B) \in \{ (5,3), (5,4), (5,5), (6,2), \ldots \}$$
and many many other examples. 
